      "title": "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "80b6edf3-8d55-527b-a810-490529a42c03",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x)=(3 x^{4} + x)^{5}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{5},\\,g=3 x^{4} + x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=5g^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=12 x^{3} + 1.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=5g^{4}\\cdot (12 x^{3} + 1) \\\\  &=5(3 x^{4} + x)^{4}(12 x^{3} + 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
